on  that ensures antagonistic muscles will  relax during the movement  this is as much about strength as it is  about dexterity  the ability to recruit larger motor  units as needed  likewise functional movement patterns  like cable chops cable punch-outs and  one-handed push-ups  can all strengthen these areas similarly  unilateral movements that involve  jumping from one leg or lifting on one  side embracing the core on the other  have huge benefit eithering unilateral  exercises will also encourage  symmetrical muscle development  and strength finally though we should  also practice movements that train both  sides simultaneously  i've spoken recently about movements  like crawls and their contralateral  nature  and how that challenges us to exert  strength and control between hemispheres  this cross-lateral movement helps us to  develop the corpus callosum  in infancy and it can be used just the  same way in adulthood  in fact any complex movement that  requires communication between the two  sides of the body  may offer these benefits other options  include juggling the aforementioned rock  climbing  tai chi yoga other certain types of  martial arts etc  we can also use more complex movements  in strength training creating hybrid  movements that require strength  to be exerted on different vectors  simultaneously  this could not only develop strength and  control but also spatial awareness  and perhaps even creativity  so i hope you found this video useful  and interesting guys if you did then  please leave a like and share around  that helps me out  immensely subscribe if you want more  like this and hit the notification  button  if you want to be notified of new  uploads let me know what you think in  the comments down below  i think ambidexterity training has the  potential to unlock unknown depths of  athletic performance  and to affect our brains in really  interesting ways is this something  you've experimented with  or will you do now if you want to lear