t to many more  notable individuals who are not  cross-dominant some suggest that  ambidexterity may correlate with lower  brain  lateralization that is to say that the  brain's structures are less neatly  organized on either side of the brain  and are more dispersed across both  hemispheres resulting in brains that are  more symmetrical among the ambidextrous  population  this may also result in a thicker corpus  callosum the bundle of nerves that  bridges the hemispheres  posthumous autopsies of einstein's brain  revealed that he did in fact possess a  thicker than average corpus callosum  and we know that women on the whole have  a naturally less symmetrical brain  which makes sense of that study we  looked at earlier with more information  traveling more freely around the brain  it is possible that there's a greater  potential for novel connections to form  between ideas  left-handers who make up approximately  ten percent of the population  as opposed to the one percent of truly  ambidextrous individuals  also tend to have less brain  lateralization this is due to the  increased need for ambidexterity  in a right-hand world a change in  lateralization  could also result in other cognitive  differences one study found that those  with left-handed relatives  deemed to be weak-handed and therefore  more ambidextrous  actually had better episodic memories  memories for events  those same individuals also demonstrated  poorer semantic memory the study author  suggests that this difference stems from  the fact that episodic memories are  coded across multiple brain areas  where semantic memories require just one  hemisphere  being able to pull information from  across the brain is useful for episodic  recall  but the crosstalk may result in unwanted  interference when it comes to semantic  information  hampering speed and accuracy those with  sinistrality  in their dna may find their thoughts  take the scenic route so to speak  it is true that there's a greater  incidence of various cognitive  disturbances in ambidextrous individuals  a number of studies link ambidexterity  to poorer iq scores  adhd schizophrenia and more  interestingly both adhd and  schizophrenia  are linked with creativity whereas iq  tests benefit from rapid processing of  information  however this correlation is unlikely to  be causative  that is to say that in some cases  ambidexterity is a symptom of  development issues  and not the reverse there is evidence  for this as we see in a review of the  literature conducted by johanna  barbara satella systematic  investigations of the second group of  subjects  always revealed perinatal cerebral  disturbances  this paper discusses the thesis the  insufficient oxygen supply to the brain  in the perinatal period of life  mainly affects the function of the  dominant cerebral hemisphere that is  responsible  for the congenital handedness  even if increased brain symmetry results  in a less organized brain it is highly  unlikely that training for ambidexterity  later in life would result in the  migration of entire brain functions  we would not likely see the brain become  significantly more symmetrical  as we simply lack that kind of  plasticity in adulthood that said we  might be able to thicken the corpus  callosum  in order to improve communication  between hemispheres  and we might see improvements in the  motor cortex and cerebellum  many people could benefit from being a  little more creative  note that the link between weak  handedness and a thicker corpus callosum  is far from proven however  so how do you train this most people  looking to improve their ambidexterity  will be writing with a non-dominant hand  other drills include tooth brushing with  the left hand swapping cutlery over etc  this is a great starting point and can  help to develop fine motor control  that will almost certainly transfer to  other tasks to some extent  in fact an interesting demonstration of  this comes from young children  our health visitor told us that our  daughter emmy would be able to brush her  own teeth  only once she'd started writing at  school it's the process of writing you  see  that develops the coordination and  control necessary to create those small  circles with the brush  we've already seen that the aerosmith  school uses similar strategies to help  improve verbal fluency  of their pupils other inherently  ambidextrous practices such as learning  to play the piano  can also have similar effects musicians  that use both hands have been shown to  have thicker corpus callosums  although this is only true for male  musicians rock climbing too may also  offer some benefits  for boosting creativity and more all  these options should prove  highly effective but transfer across  all skills is not a given one study  found that our non-dominant hands are  actually superior when it comes to  holding things steady  this makes a lot of sense given that we  so often hold something in one hand  and manipulate it with the other the  image of a bow and arrow comes to mind  this also suggests that through practice  we've improved different types of skill  on each hand this suggests that we need  a broader approach  many of us are so lacking in nambi  dexterity that any additional training  will benefit us  to an extent but just as practicing  cursive with your right hand is only  going to improve your right hook to a  certain degree  so too can we get more benefit with  specific training on the left side  in other words if you want to get better  at punching with your left hand you  should practice  punching with your left hand keep in  mind that this is a whole body movement  that involves coordination of the  shoulders fists hips and more  right now you probably lack the  necessary motor mats to engage all those  motor units  in the right sequence and with the same  explosiveness you enjoy on your dominant  side  you likewise also probably struggle to  achieve the reciprocal inhibition  that ensures antagonistic muscles will  relax during the movement  this is as much about strength as it is  about dexterity  the ability to recruit larger motor  units as needed  likewise functional movement patterns  like cable chops cable punch-outs and  one-handed push-ups  can all strengthen these areas similarly  unilateral movements that involve  jumping from one leg or lifting on one  side embracing the core on the other  have huge benefit eithering unilateral  exercises will also encourage  symmetrical muscle development  and strength finally though we should  also practice movements that train both  sides simultaneously  i've spoken recently about movements  like crawls and their contralateral  nature  and how that challenges us to exert  strength and control between hemispheres  this cross-lateral movement helps us to  develop the corpus callosum  in infancy and it can be used just the  same way in adulthood  in fact any complex movement that  requires communication between the two  sides of the body  may offer these benefits other options  include juggling the aforementioned rock  climbing  tai chi yoga other certain types of  martial arts etc  we can also use more complex movements  in strength training creating hybrid  movements that require strength  to be exerted on different vectors  simultaneously  this could not only develop strength and  control but also spatial awareness  and perhaps even creativity  so i hope you found this video useful  and interesting guys if you did then  please leave a like and share around  that helps me out  immensely subscribe if you want more  like this and hit the notification  button  if you want to be notified of new  uploads let me know what you think in  the comments down below  i think ambidexterity training has the  potential to unlock unknown depths of  athletic performance  and to affect our brains in really  interesting ways is this something  you've experimented with  or will you do now if you want to learn  more about training your body in  all kinds of different ways all at once  to become as functional as possible then  check out my ebook and training program  super functional training i'll leave a  link to that in the description down  below  and there's a discount on right now  whilst we're all in lockdown  alternatively you can check out my print  book functional training and beyond  which will take you on a journey through  the history of functional training and  all the different  forms of training you can apply to  create a more well-rounded program  yourself  either way thanks so much for watching  this one guys thank you for all your  support  and i'll see you next time bye  for now


